{
  "families": [
    {
      "budget_share": 0.6,
      "family": "iterative",
      "ranges": [
        {"choices": ["trunc", "round"], "high": 4, "low": 0, "param": "frac_guard"},
        {"choices": ["trunc", "round"], "high": 4, "low": -2, "param": "angle_guard"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 16, "low": 12, "param": "n_iter"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 18, "low": 12, "param": "data_width"}
      ],
      "why": "iterative is the lowest-area family (single datapath, no pipeline registers). For >=1 MSPS it needs Fmax >= 1 MHz, which is trivially met. The area/accuracy trade-off is driven by data_width and n_iter; frac_guard and angle_guard add area for marginal accuracy. Focusing n_iter 12-16 and data_width 12-18 covers the region where max_abs_err <= 2^-10 is achievable at minimal area."
    },
    {
      "budget_share": 0.4,
      "family": "unrolled_k",
      "ranges": [
        {"choices": ["trunc", "round"], "high": 4, "low": 0, "param": "frac_guard"},
        {"choices": ["trunc", "round"], "high": 4, "low": -2, "param": "angle_guard"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 16, "low": 12, "param": "n_iter"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 18, "low": 12, "param": "data_width"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 4, "low": 2, "param": "k"}
      ],
      "why": "unrolled_k with k=2..4 reduces latency and may allow a slightly higher Fmax, but adds area from duplicated datapath logic. Since area is the primary objective, this family is less likely to beat iterative, but k=2 with fewer iterations could offer a different point on the Pareto front. Limited budget share since iterative is expected to dominate on area."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec demands >=1 MSPS throughput and max_abs_err <= 2^-10, with area as the primary objective. Throughput is easily met by both iterative and unrolled_k since Fmax will be well above 1 MHz. The key trade-off is between area (minimize data_width, n_iter, guards) and accuracy (need enough iterations and bits to hit 2^-10 error). I focus data_width 12-18 and n_iter 12-16 because these are the parameters that most directly control both area and accuracy. frac_guard and angle_guard are kept in their full ranges to let the optimizer find the minimal guard values that still meet accuracy. iterative gets 60% of budget as it is the lowest-area family; unrolled_k gets 40% to explore whether unrolling can trade a small area increase for a better accuracy/area point on the Pareto front."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "next_plan": null,
  "rationale": "The spec is fully satisfied with a large margin on the critical constraint. The Pareto front contains 11 feasible designs, all meeting throughput >= 1 MSPS (best 10.1 MSPS) and max_abs_err <= 2^-10 (best 4.81e-05 = 2^-14.34, well below the 9.77e-04 limit). The minimum area design is the iterative CORDIC with data_width=15, n_iter=14, angle_guard=1, frac_guard=2, truncation, at 7405 um^2 with 11.1 accuracy bits and 8 MSPS throughput. This is far below the HV reference of 40000 um^2. The front spans a reasonable trade-off curve from 7405 um^2/11.1 bits to 13847 um^2/14.3 bits, covering the accuracy range from just above the 10-bit reference to well beyond it. The iterative family dominates on area (7405 vs 12183 best for unrolled_k), and the pipelined families were not needed since the iterative architecture already achieves >1 MSPS throughput with the lowest area. The selection rule (min area) picks the 7405 um^2 design, which is robustly feasible. No further exploration would improve the hypervolume meaningfully given the budget is exhausted and the front is well-mapped in the relevant region."
}
